"Epictetus began his handbook with the division between what is in our power and what is not, and placed the source of distress in our opinions. Men are disturbed not by things, but by the views which they take of things. Pain is an event in the body. That it is terrible is a judgment, and a judgment can be withheld. This is the simile of the two arrows in another idiom, and the Stoic exercises for withholding assent resemble Buddhist training in watching feeling without reacting. The Stoics differed in holding that the fault is one of reasoning and that the remedy is to reason correctly, in agreement with a rational universe. The Buddha placed the root deeper than opinion, in a thirst that is felt before it is thought, and he prescribed attention to it more than argument against it.": "3bc5152e644d",
"Epicurus taught in a garden in Athens that philosophy is medicine for the soul, and that most human misery comes from wanting the wrong things. He sorted desires into three kinds. Some are natural and necessary, like the desire for food and shelter, and these are easily met. Some are natural and unnecessary, like the desire for fine food. Some are neither, like the desires for fame and power, and these can never be satisfied because they have no limit. The cure is to keep to the first kind. By pleasure we mean the absence of pain in the body and of trouble in the soul. This is close to the Buddha in method. Both treat desire as something to be examined and trained, and both locate the trouble in desires that have no natural end. Epicurus kept pleasure as the goal, and he kept the self that enjoys it. He promised his followers a tranquil life and told them that death is nothing to us. He saw no thirst for existence that needed uprooting.": "bf8ab21e7307",
"In his first discourse the Buddha stated what he called the noble truth of suffering. Rebirth is suffering; old age is suffering; illness is suffering; death is suffering; being coupled with the disliked is suffering; separation from the liked is suffering; not getting what you wish for is suffering. In brief, the five grasping aggregates are suffering. The list does not say that life is suffering. It names particular things, and it ends with a summary that carries the weight: the aggregates as grasped. The Pali word, dukkha, is wider than pain. Elsewhere the Buddha distinguished three forms: the suffering inherent in painful feeling; the suffering inherent in conditions; and the suffering inherent in perishing. The first is what everyone means by suffering. The third is the unsatisfactoriness of pleasant things, which end. The second is the unreliability of anything put together by conditions. He never denied that life contains pleasure, and he spoke of it often.": "8c157c6fafe9",
"Nearly every Western listener has heard that the Buddha taught that life is suffering and that the answer is to give up desire. Both halves are wrong, and together they make Buddhism sound like a philosophy for the defeated. Correct the first by reading the actual list from the first discourse, which names particular things, and by explaining that the word covers the plain fact that pleasant things end. Correct the second with the word thirst. Everyone knows the difference between enjoying a meal and being unable to stop eating. The two arrows are the best single image to offer. They concede at once that pain is real, which listeners need to hear, and they point to the part of suffering that is added and can be removed.": "0b45fc745faa",
"On the conclusion Schopenhauer is closest. He alone among Western philosophers made suffering the starting point of his thought and traced it to wanting as such. Buddhists in Europe were for a long time read through him, which is one source of the belief that Buddhism is pessimistic. On the argument Epicurus and the Stoics are closer than he is, because they share the Buddhas assumption that desire can be worked on. On the purpose all three Greek and Roman schools agree with the Buddha that philosophy exists to relieve suffering. This is the widest agreement between ancient Western philosophy and the Dharma, and the next lesson takes it up.": "d84d35d6a86d",
"Pain is the first arrow. What we add to it is the second, and that one is optional.": "c53fdeb76b60",
"Pleasure is real. The trouble starts when we demand that it stay.": "6b85e82d4457",
"Schopenhauer agreed with the Buddha about the disease. He did not think there was a cure. The Buddha did.": "b148c7823c55",
"Schopenhauer held that the inner nature of everything, ourselves included, is a blind striving that he called will, and that to be a creature of will is to suffer. Wanting is lack, and lack is pain. When a want is met, the relief is brief. No attained object of desire can give lasting satisfaction, but merely a fleeting gratification; it is like the alms thrown to the beggar, that keeps him alive to-day that his misery may be prolonged till the morrow. When no want presses, boredom arrives, so that life swings like a pendulum backwards and forwards between pain and ennui. No Western philosopher has described the second noble truth so exactly, and he reached it before he had read much about Buddhism. He was glad to find the agreement afterward. The difference lies in what the will is. For Schopenhauer it is the thing in itself, the one reality behind appearances, and no one can be rid of what he is. Release comes only to rare saints who deny the will to live. He admired them and did not claim to be one.": "86947e39c85e",
"The Buddha did not say that life is suffering. He listed the things that hurt, and then asked why they hurt as much as they do.": "6e7a672c4311",
"The Buddha explained how craving produces suffering with a simile. When an untrained person feels bodily pain, they sorrow and wail and lament, and so they experience two feelings: physical and mental. Its like a person who is struck with an arrow, only to be struck with a second arrow. The first arrow is the pain. The second is the resistance to it, and the search for pleasure as the only escape the person knows. A trained person feels the first arrow and not the second. Another image makes the point about pleasure. A man with leprosy scratches his sores and holds his limbs over a pit of coals, and finds relief in it. Cured, he would not envy another leper the same relief. The pleasure was real, and it belonged to the disease.": "2effffaef476",
"The first further step is a distinction the Western schools did not draw in the same place. The Buddha did not teach that desire should be extinguished. He taught that thirst should be. The tradition has a separate word, chanda, for the wish to do something, and it speaks of a wholesome form of it: the wish to practice, to understand, to help. Without that wish no one would set out on the path. The mark of thirst is that it clings and cannot bear to be refused. The mark of wholesome wishing is that it can act fully and accept the result. A parent who works for a childs good and a person who cannot stop checking a telephone both want something. The teaching addresses the second and relies on the first. Schopenhauer, who put all wanting under one name, had no room for the difference, and so for him the only alternative to suffering was the stilling of life itself.": "d3a4c6e6f887",
"The likeness ends in a different place for each. With Schopenhauer it ends at the third noble truth. He has the diagnosis without the prognosis, and his denial of the will looks, from the Buddhist side, like the craving for nonexistence that the second truth lists as part of the disease. With Epicurus it ends at the self. He reduces desires and leaves the one who desires in place. With the Stoics it ends at reason. They trust the faculty that the Buddha thought was itself moved by thirst. The Buddhas account is the only one of the four in which the cure is complete and the person who is cured is not what he took himself to be.": "4caf01b07f20",
"The path itself runs on a kind of wanting: the wish to understand and to be of use.": "b7a40b304362",
"The second step concerns depth. Epicurus and the Stoics addressed the desires and opinions that a person can find by reflection. The Buddhas list of cravings begins there, with sensual pleasure, and goes on to the craving to be, and the craving not to be. These are not desires for any object. They are the minds insistence on being someone and its recoil from being that one. On his account they lie under the others and feed them, and no sorting of desires into necessary and unnecessary reaches them. They are reached by the kind of observation described in the lessons on not-self and impermanence, in which the one who thirsts is looked for and not found. For this reason the first two noble truths cannot be separated from the rest of the teaching. The cure for thirst turns out to be a discovery about who was thirsty.": "9a9f489f4548",
"The second truth names the origin. Its the craving that leads to future lives, mixed up with relishing and greed, taking pleasure there wherever it alights. That is, craving for sensual pleasures, craving for continued existence, and craving for nonexistence. The word translated craving is taṇhā, which means thirst. It is a narrower thing than desire in general. A person may wish to help a friend or to finish a piece of work without thirst in this sense. Craving is the demand that experience be otherwise than it is: that the pleasant stay, that the unpleasant go, that I continue, or that I be rid of myself. The third of these is easily overlooked. The wish to be annihilated is counted as a form of craving. It is not treated as its cure.": "84a2a11328f1",
"The word is thirst, and it is narrower than desire. You can want something without being unable to bear its absence.": "0b7932f65d70"
}
